name attack-ccrp-1
timeout 200

# READING-RULE UPDATE (model delta D1, flaperon fix): this mission currently ends TIMEOUT, exit 3.
# The run-in roll no longer produces a lift step, release vertical velocity flipped sign, and the
# fire-control's own table-vs-aero prediction error (53-64 m, a documented intentional property)
# stopped CANCELLING the aim error and started adding (aim error now ~80 m vs the ~45 m the target
# requires). This surfaced a PRE-EXISTING weakness that used to pass by cancellation; a fire-control
# accuracy round is queued (see doc/flightbox/journal.md). Until it lands, TIMEOUT is the expected
# outcome here and NOT a delivery regression marker.

# THE CCRP DELIVERY, end to end: the pilot flies a level laydown at a declared ground target, the fire
# control computes when to let go, the round is released over the command bus on that cue, and the
# target is destroyed by the burst its impact makes. `objective kill unit bunker` is what turns all of
# that into a verdict — exit 0 only if the thing on the ground stopped standing.
#
# WHY LEVEL AND NOT A DIVE. FBAutopilot::Direct holds an altitude and steers to a point, so a straight
# level run-in is a trajectory this simulator flies EXACTLY and repeatably. A 20-30 degree dive would be
# the pilot fighting its own altitude hold the whole way down the chute, and every metre of miss would
# then be an argument about the flying rather than a measurement of the delivery computation. A laydown
# makes the release MOMENT the only variable — which is the point of the mission (and of its sibling
# attack-late.fbm, where that moment is deliberately wrong).
#
# RUN THE MEASUREMENT WITH `--elev const`. With no `runway` line that provider is a flat 0 m base
# everywhere (core/FBRunwayPlateauElevation), so the fire control's impact plane, the target's own
# elevation and the ground the physics judge resolves the impact against are all the same number, and
# the `stores DELIVERY` line's predErrM is purely what the COMPUTER got wrong: its coarse stored
# ballistic table (core/FBStore.h's kMk82 Perf) against the vendored model's own Mach-dependent
# aerodynamics. Run it on the default swiss DEM and it still flies and still kills — the prediction then
# also carries the terrain difference between the ground under the JET (which is what a barometric
# ranging solution knows) and the ground at the impact point, which is a real error of the real method.
#
# WHAT THE RUN PRODUCES, all in events.log:
#   pilot ATTACK_RELEASE   the cue the pilot released on: time-to-release, along/cross error, bomb range
#   sms RELEASE_SOLUTION   what the computer predicted, as it left the jet on the round
#   stores IMPACT          where it actually landed, measured by the physics judge
#   stores DELIVERY        the two put together: predErrM (computer) and aimErrM (delivery)
#   damage DAMAGE/KILL     what the burst did to the bunker
#
# GEOMETRY: 19 km of straight run-in at 900 m and 450 KCAS, target dead ahead on the nose. The
# altitude is a delivery decision and it is the one that matters most: every degree of track error at
# release becomes a lateral miss proportional to the bomb's throw, and the throw is set by the height.

unit viper
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 46.90000 6.80000  900 90.0 450       # due east at the target, co-track, 19 km out
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set datalink off                           # nothing here is an air engagement
  set fcr_mode off
  set store 3 mk82                           # left wing pylon
  set store 7 mk82                           # its mirror — the second is never dropped: one pass
  set brief_master_arm arm                   # entered over the bus once airborne, like every brief item
  set task attack
  set attack_mode ccrp
  wp 46.90000 7.05000 900 450                # THE TARGET: the active steerpoint IS the aim point
  wp 46.95000 7.20000 3000 400               # the egress point, flown after the break turn
  # THE VERDICT IS THE TARGET, and nothing else: the run ends the tick the bunker stops being
  # combat-effective. Deliberately NOT `objective waypoints` as well — a strike waypoint is a point you
  # aim at from three kilometres away, not one you arrive at, and this jet releases well short of it and
  # then turns away. attack-ccip.fbm is the mission that carries the pass through to its end.
  objective kill unit bunker

unit bunker
  module target_soft                         # modules/ground: an unprotected installation, no FDM
  team hostile
  spawn 46.90000 7.05000 ground 90.0 0       # sits where the steerpoint is; heading = its long axis
